2026-06-30 15:00:53,949 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-ac6bb455-250d-4581-a67d-f32b944f0f4b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nE18788_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_Mock-1.raw\nE18790_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_Mock-2.raw\nE18792_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_Mock-3.raw\nE18794_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_T2A-1.raw\nE18796_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_T2A-2.raw\nE18800_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_T2A-3.raw\nE18802_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_T2A_intact-1.raw\nE18804_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_T2A_intact-2.raw\nE18806_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-323_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_OE-T2A_T2A_intact-3.raw\nE18814_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_WT_I1078_rep1.raw\nE18816_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_WT_I1078_rep2.raw\nE18818_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_WT_I1078_rep3.raw\nE18820_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_C12-E6_I100_rep1.raw\nE18822_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_C12-E6_I100_rep2.raw\nE18824_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_C12-E6_I100_rep3.raw\nE18826_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_C12-E6_I1078_rep1.raw\nE18828_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_C12-E6_I1078_rep2.raw\nE18830_1p1_9_20cm_CMB-328_SVEYC_GielVDM_APMS_C12-E6_I1078_rep3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nA well-controlled BioID design for endogenous bait proteins\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe CRISPR/Cas9 revolution is profoundly changing the way life sciences technologies are used. Many assays now rely on engineered clonal cell lines to eliminate overexpression issues. Control cell lines are typically provided by non-engineered cells or by engineered clones implying a considerable risk for artefacts because of clonal variation. Genome engineering can also be expected to transform BioID , an elegant proximity labeling method that relies on fusing a bait protein to a promiscuous biotin ligase, BirA*, which results in the tagging of vicinal proteins. We here propose an innovative design to enable BioID for endogenous proteins. To enable this design wherein, we introduced a T2A-BirA* module at the C-terminus of endogenous p53 by genome engineering. This leads to bi-cistronic expression of both p53 and BirA* under control of the endogenous promoter ensuring low background biotinylation in these control cells. By targeting a Cas9-cytidine deaminase base editor to the T2A auto-cleavage site, we can efficiently derive an isogenic population expressing a functional p53-BirA* fusion protein. By A quantitative proteomics workflow we show significant benefits over other experimental settings including forced expression of p53-BirA* and parental control cell lines, and we provide a  enabled a ffirst well-controlled view on the proximal proteins of endogenous p53. This novel application for base editors expands the CRISPR/Cas9 toolbox and may prove to be a valuable addition for synthetic biology.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor every sample three 145 cm2 culture dishes (Nunc) were seeded with 4.5x106 HCT116 TP53+/T2A-BirA* cells cultured on McCoy’s 5A modified medium supplemented with dialyzed FBS. 24 h after seeding 1 µM doxorubicin was added. To every culture dish biotin was added to a final concentration of 50 µM at the start of the treatment. 24 h after treatment the growth medium was replenished to deprive free biotin from the culture. 3 h later, the growth medium was aspirated and cells were washed once with cold PBS before detachment in PBS using a cell scraper. After pelleting cells by centrifugation for 5 min at 500 g on 4°C, a second PBS washing step occurred prior to cell lysis in a 15 ml tube. 2 ml of ice-cold RIPA lysis buffer containing 250 U of benzonase (Sigma, E1014) was added to each pellet. Lysis was obtained by incubation with agitation for 1 h on 4°C prior to sonication (30% amplitude, 5 x 6 s burst, 2 s interruption) on ice. The insoluble fraction of the lysate was removed by centrifugation for 15 min at 16000 g on 4°C and the supernatant was transferred to a new 15 ml tube. The total protein concentration was determined by the Bradford protein assay to normalize input material to the maximum shared total protein amount throughout the samples. For every sample, 90 µl Streptavidin Sepharose High Performance bead suspension (GE Healthcare, 17-5113-01) was used for enrichment of biotinylated proteins. Beads were pelleted by centrifugation at 500 g for 1 min and washed once in wash buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 1% NP-40, 1 mM EDTA, 1 mM EGTA, 0,1% SDS) prior to addition to the lysate. Samples were incubated for affinity purification on a rotator at 4°C for 3 h. Afterwards, beads were recovered by centrifugation of the samples at 500 g for 1 min at 4 °C and aspirating the supernatant. Beads were washed three times in wash buffer, twice in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate pH 8.0 and once with 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 2 mM CaCl2 prior to resuspension in 20 µl 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0. Trypsin digest occurred overnight by addition of 1 µg trypsin (Promega, V5111) to each sample. Digestion mixtures were depleted of beads upon centrifugation at 500 g for 1 min, after which the supernatant was transferred to a mass spectrometry vial. To ensure complete digestion, peptide mixtures were incubated with an additional 500 ng trypsin for 3 h before addition of formic acid to a final concentration of 2%. All experiments were performed in biological triplicate for downstream label-free quantitative proteome analysis.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
