2026-06-30 15:01:16,221 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e4e38e8b-1750-4a37-86c3-2899531702b4', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA well-controlled BioID design for endogenous bait proteins\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe CRISPR/Cas9 revolution is profoundly changing the way life sciences technologies are used. Many assays now rely on engineered clonal cell lines to eliminate overexpression issues. Control cell lines are typically provided by non-engineered cells or by engineered clones implying a considerable risk for artefacts because of clonal variation. Genome engineering can also be expected to transform BioID , an elegant proximity labeling method that relies on fusing a bait protein to a promiscuous biotin ligase, BirA*, which results in the tagging of vicinal proteins. We here propose an innovative design to enable BioID for endogenous proteins. To enable this design wherein, we introduced a T2A-BirA* module at the C-terminus of endogenous p53 by genome engineering. This leads to bi-cistronic expression of both p53 and BirA* under control of the endogenous promoter ensuring low background biotinylation in these control cells. By targeting a Cas9-cytidine deaminase base editor to the T2A auto-cleavage site, we can efficiently derive an isogenic population expressing a functional p53-BirA* fusion protein. By A quantitative proteomics workflow we show significant benefits over other experimental settings including forced expression of p53-BirA* and parental control cell lines, and we provide a  enabled a ffirst well-controlled view on the proximal proteins of endogenous p53. This novel application for base editors expands the CRISPR/Cas9 toolbox and may prove to be a valuable addition for synthetic biology.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor every sample three 145 cm2 culture dishes (Nunc) were seeded with 4.5x106 HCT116 TP53+/T2A-BirA* cells cultured on McCoy’s 5A modified medium supplemented with dialyzed FBS. 24 h after seeding 1 µM doxorubicin was added. To every culture dish biotin was added to a final concentration of 50 µM at the start of the treatment. 24 h after treatment the growth medium was replenished to deprive free biotin from the culture. 3 h later, the growth medium was aspirated and cells were washed once with cold PBS before detachment in PBS using a cell scraper. After pelleting cells by centrifugation for 5 min at 500 g on 4°C, a second PBS washing step occurred prior to cell lysis in a 15 ml tube. 2 ml of ice-cold RIPA lysis buffer containing 250 U of benzonase (Sigma, E1014) was added to each pellet. Lysis was obtained by incubation with agitation for 1 h on 4°C prior to sonication (30% amplitude, 5 x 6 s burst, 2 s interruption) on ice. The insoluble fraction of the lysate was removed by centrifugation for 15 min at 16000 g on 4°C and the supernatant was transferred to a new 15 ml tube. The total protein concentration was determined by the Bradford protein assay to normalize input material to the maximum shared total protein amount throughout the samples. For every sample, 90 µl Streptavidin Sepharose High Performance bead suspension (GE Healthcare, 17-5113-01) was used for enrichment of biotinylated proteins. Beads were pelleted by centrifugation at 500 g for 1 min and washed once in wash buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 1% NP-40, 1 mM EDTA, 1 mM EGTA, 0,1% SDS) prior to addition to the lysate. Samples were incubated for affinity purification on a rotator at 4°C for 3 h. Afterwards, beads were recovered by centrifugation of the samples at 500 g for 1 min at 4 °C and aspirating the supernatant. Beads were washed three times in wash buffer, twice in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate pH 8.0 and once with 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0, 2 mM CaCl2 prior to resuspension in 20 µl 20 mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0. Trypsin digest occurred overnight by addition of 1 µg trypsin (Promega, V5111) to each sample. Digestion mixtures were depleted of beads upon centrifugation at 500 g for 1 min, after which the supernatant was transferred to a mass spectrometry vial. To ensure complete digestion, peptide mixtures were incubated with an additional 500 ng trypsin for 3 h before addition of formic acid to a final concentration of 2%. All experiments were performed in biological triplicate for downstream label-free quantitative proteome analysis.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
